A||||7||pHoOl||Broken Engine belt||||1||
Z||000000||pHoOl||04-22-2001||08:22 PM||||Engine belt went up in a large puff of smoke yesterday.  I don't exactly have the means to fix it myself in the parking lot of my college.  Planning on taking it to a Pontiac dealer to do it.  The question is.... how much should it cost?  I'm assuming no more than like 20 for the belt, and they'll probably demand 50 to put it in.  <P>Does this sound about right?<BR><P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who can't outrun the cops.<P>AIM: phool88fiero<P>    <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/pHoOl_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Bob Englert||04-22-2001||08:42 PM||||Be VERY careful how you intend to get the car to the dealership to have them put the new belt on.  You could easily overheat the motor if your water pump is not working due to the broken belt.  <P>You don't say what year or engine you have, but some of the belts can be installed with pretty basic hand tools.<P>Good luck...<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||04-22-2001||09:40 PM||||Belts are EASY to put on. Just take off the rear passenger wheel, take a 13mm socket and ratchet, loosen up the alternator locking bolt, slap the belt on, and using a 24" or longer jack handle, wedge the alternator back(disconnect battery first) and tighten the bolt with your free hand. Might be something that takes some time first time, but is very easy. I've got it down to just a few minutes.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ronnie||04-22-2001||10:34 PM||||When I went to pick up the 88GT a couple of weeks ago, the belt broke with me coming home. I stopped at a little jobshop garage, and they took me to Autozone, got the belt for $9.99, and they installed it for me for $25 bucks. But it can be changed with regular sockets & a pry bar of some sort, without taking the rear tire off.This is of course, for the V6.<P>------------------<BR>'86GT/'88FORMULA/'88GT<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ronnies_1.jpg"> ||||reg||6
Z||000004||pHoOl||04-22-2001||10:41 PM||||Mine's an 88 V6.  Sounds easy, however, since i don't have the tools or the space to do it, i might just be better off fixing it myself next time.  <P>I was going to tow it to the dealer.  It got from normal to 220 degrees before going all the way across campus when it broke, and the nearest dealer is 7 miles.  But, i get free towing..... gotta love AAA.<P><P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who can't outrun the cops.<P>AIM: phool88fiero<P>    <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/pHoOl_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||fyerstarter||04-22-2001||10:50 PM||||You could just get a ride to autozone and pick up the tools you need for next to nothing. And changing the belt really is easy, I don't think there is anyone who can't do it. If you do decide to take it to a shop I would prob steer clear of the Pontiac dealer, they like to charge outrageous proces for even the smallest job. Any shop will be able to do this for you, even most tire shops and oil change places do small repairs like that.<P>Good luck||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||04-22-2001||10:53 PM||||IF You actually smoked a belt..... that usually means the water pump or alternator has serious problems. Simple belt failure usually doesn't make them smoke. Usually just breaks and falls off. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||JR||04-22-2001||11:25 PM||||Only belt I smoked was the A/C belt when the compressor seized, while on 401 returning home from Toronto.  If it was alternator belt wouldn't you hear a squealing from slip first, then possibly smoke?  Theogre's probably right though, bearings, check that the alternator pulley actually turns as well as waterpump.  ||||reg||1
